WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.549
Film Courage: What’s the first step in writing
a screenplay, either for television or feature

00:00:04.549 --> 00:00:05.549
film?

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:09.730
Daniel Calvisi: Well, I think it’s making
sure that your concept can sustain a full

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.790
feature film or a series.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:17.440
Now in TV it’s trickier here because you
not only need to have a great pilot (which

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:23.340
is one episode of your show) but you need
to have a concept that can sustain a series

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.210
for multiple seasons.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:30.480
So you need to have what a friend of mine
called a playground (a dramatic playground)

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.989
which is you create this very specific world
and then you put these very interesting characters

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:41.350
into it to play and bang against each other
and have lots of dramatic conflict.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:46.010
If that idea is something that can sustain
and run for awhile, maybe that’s a better

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.370
idea for TV.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:53.609
Whereas feature films have closed endings,
so you are going to have an idea with a beginning,

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:54.609
middle and end.

00:00:54.609 --> 00:00:55.789
And that’s pretty much that.

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:57.100
Film Courage: Okay.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.060
Let’s take the feature film scenario.

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:03.120
So [what is] the first step in writing a screenplay
for a feature film?

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.010
Daniel Calvisi: I would say story mapping
it out.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:10.320
If you have a concept that you think is great
and you’re passionate about it and you want

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.189
to write it, write a story map (which is your
basic outline).

00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:20.780
So it’s going to be for 8 to 12 major signpost
beats and your 4 story engines and you’re

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.289
going to define your core elements first (what
I call the basic story map), which is your

00:01:25.289 --> 00:01:30.649
protagonist, their external and internal goal,
the main dramatic conflict standing in the

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:34.820
way of that goal that is going to generate
all those obstacles that are really going

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.030
to sustain the story.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:42.240
Your controlling theme which is your major
meta theme that you are going to be exploring,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.130
and then a few other elements like your protagonist.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:54.130
You want to have your skill, their misbehavior
(a misbehavior is a quirk or personality trait

00:01:54.130 --> 00:02:00.170
that consistently generates conflict) and
then their achilles heel which is just that

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.710
thing that could destroy them, could potentially
destroy them.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:08.800
So once you have those core elements and then
you have the major signpost beats of your

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:14.530
outline, then you are ready to do a full scene
outline and then jump in and write pages.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:19.070
Film Courage: What is the first step in writing
a screenplay for a TV series?

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:24.670
Daniel Calvisi: I would always say start the
story as late as possible, enter the story

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:30.420
as late as possible so you don’t have much
set-up and so the story can get going right

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.420
away.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:38.370
Film Courage: Do you think sometimes new writers
they overwrite and it’s too dense and they

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:40.400
are giving us too much backstory?

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.590
Daniel Calvisi: Yeah…too much set-up.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:43.900
You don’t need that much set-up.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.800
Maybe you’re opening with a flash forward
like in Breaking Bad.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:47.800
It’s like BOOM!

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:52.770
- we are in this intense scene where there
is this guy in his underwear driving an RV

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.850
like a bat out of hell and he has a gas mask
on.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:03:00.230
And he crashes the RV and in the script he
(Brian Cranston) was called underwear man

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:04.180
before we find out his character’s name
was Walter White.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.400
But they just BOOM!

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.970
dropped us right into the action and that
is something that really grabs a reader.

